{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["# Day 20 — Pandas merging & joins\n","Objectives:\n","- Inner, left, right, outer joins.\n","- Handling key collisions and suffixes.\n","- Efficient merges and memory tips."]},{"cell_type":"code","metadata":{},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[],"source":["import pandas as pd\n","customers = pd.DataFrame({\"cid\":[1,2,3], \"name\":[\"Ada\",\"Alan\",\"Linus\"]})\n","orders = pd.DataFrame({\"oid\":[10,11,12], \"cid\":[1,1,4], \"amount\":[100,200,300]})\n","customers, orders\n","\n","pd.merge(customers, orders, on='cid', how='inner')\n","pd.merge(customers, orders, on='cid', how='left', suffixes=('_c','_o'))\n","pd.merge(customers, orders, on='cid', how='outer')\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["## Exercises\n","1) Create products and order_items tables and join to compute revenue by customer.\n","2) Demonstrate right join and explain when it’s useful.\n","3) Use validate='one_to_many' to catch unexpected duplicates."]}]}
